      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "The effective management of cancer pain is one of the greatest challenges for GPs."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "Pain management can be best achieved with a knowledge of the fundamentals of cancer pain and its assessment. A systematic and holistic approach to its treatment needs to be individualised for each patient, taking into account the relevant physical, psychological and spiritual factors."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "DISCUSSION",
          "content": "GPs are part of a team including oncologists, palliative care specialists, nursing services, paramedical staff, counsellors and clergy and so should know about and make use of the ever-improving support services which are available to ensure that insoluble pain problems are a rare occurrence."
        }
      ]},
